Binkley's Calendar Program

Update: Oct. 26 1987; Copyright (C) Peter Binkley, 1987

Version 3.3   This information replaces some of the information listed below
under Version 3.2.

    Improvements in Version 3.3:

        - the program no longer uses that hideous yellow on colour monitors.
        - the speed of the program in displaying some information (especially
monthly calendars) has been improved.
        - the datafiles have been amalgamated.  The information should be in
the same format as before (described below), but the structure of the file is
this:
        \regnal
        [ listing of regnal years, as below ]
        \end
        \feast
        [ listing of feast days, as below ]
        \end
Regnal years and feast days must each be in one block, but the order of the
blocks does not matter.  There may be any amount of material before the first
'\' line.  The '\' must be at the beginning of the line.  The default name
for the data file is 'CALENDAR.DAT'; if you wish to load a different data file,
put its name as the first (and only) parameter (e.g. 'CALENDAR YORK.DAT').
       - a list of the feast days now loaded may be displayed (type 'F' on the
year menu).  The feast days are displayed in the order in which they were
loaded; for this reason, it is a good idea to alphabetize your list of feast
days.
       - Corpus Christi day appears on monthly calendars only after 1264 (the
year in which this feast was instituted.}

Version 3.2                         Aug 27, '87

Copyright (C) Peter Binkley, 1987
This program may be freely copied and shared, but is not to be sold without
the author's permission.

This program is a supplement to C. R. Cheney's Handbook for Students of
English History, Royal Historical Society Guides and Handbooks No. 4 (London,
1945; rpt. with corrections 1981).  The user simply enters the year in
question; the program then indicates the Indiction, produces a table of the
Sundays of the year with their position in the liturgical year, and finally
shows the regnal year of the English monarch or the pontifical year of the
Pope.  The user may then request a calendar of any month in that year.  The
monthly calendar is laid out in the form of modern calendars, with the Sundays
marked according to their liturgical position, and each day marked with its
position in the Roman calendar.  Using the PrtSc key, the user may obtain a
copy of the yearly table or of any monthly calendar: simply hold down the
Shift key and press PrtSc.  If the user has any questions about the medieval
calendar itself, Cheney's book should be consulted.  The file may be edited
using Nota Bene or another word processor capable of handling pure ASCII text;
this is sometimes called 'program-editing' or 'non-document' mode.

This program is good for years between 500 and 1582 A.D.  English regnal years
for William I to Henry VII (1066-1509) and pontifical years for Alexander II
to Alexander VI (1061-1503) are included.

Abbreviations:
   Anniv.      Anniversary
   Cor.        Coronation
   Depos.      Deposition
   Elec.       Election
   Resign.     Resignation
   Restor.     Restoration
   Resum.      Resumption
Antipopes are marked with an '@'.

In version 3.1 I have added the ability to include fixed feasts on the monthly
calendars.  A list of fixed feasts may be kept in a file named CAL-FST.DAT,
which must be in the current directory when the program is run.  The feasts
are listed in the following format: 'Christmas=25/12', i.e. Name=Day/Month.
No more than one feast should be on a line.  The name may be up to 10
characters long.  Any number of feasts may be in the list; however, no more
than three feasts will be remembered for a given day.  If that day happens to
fall on a Sunday, the Sunday name will be shown first and no more than two
other names will be shown; indeed, any other names are discarded and lost
until the program is restarted.  I've provided a copy of CAL-FST.DAT with a
few of the more commonly used feasts; for more, see Cheney pp. 43-64.

In version 3.2, I've allowed the user to determine what series of regnal years
will be available.  The series are kept in a file called CAL-REG.DAT, which
must in the current directory when the program is run.  There may be up to 10
series.  Each series should begin with a line starting with a '#', indicating
the name of the series: e.g. '#Kings of England'.  Then follows the list of
rulers, in order of starting date, in this format:

     William I:     Cor 25/12/1066   Death 9/9/1087'

i.e. 'Name: StartType Day/Month/Year EndType Day/Month/Year'.  The names may
be up to 13 characters long.  Acceptable StartTypes (i.e. ways of beginning a
reign) are: 'Cor': Coronation; 'Elec': Election; 'Resum': Resumption;
'Restor': Restoration; or simply 'Start'.  The user may assign these at will;
their only role is to appear on the screen in the appropriate years. 
Acceptable EndTypes are: 'Death'; 'Depos': Deposed; 'Resign': Resignation;
'Ended'.  When a reign started or ended in a given month but on an unknown
day, list the day as '0' (zero).  If a reign's anniversary was celebrated on
Ascension day (a moveable feast), as was King John's, enter the start day as
'Ascension/1099'.  Ascension is the only feast for which this will work.  If
a ruler was deposed and then restored, indicate it in this way: first put a
line indicating the very beginning and very end of his reign; then, in a
second line with the name '*', give the period of the suppression of his
rule.  For example, for Henry VI of England, the entry looks like this:

    Henry VI:      Cor 1/9/1422     Depos 11/4/1471
    *:             Restor 4/3/1461  Depos 0/9/1470

He was crowned in 1422, deposed in 1461, restored in 1470 and deposed in 1471. 
The second line appears confusing, for it puts the StartType (which refers to
the manner of his restoration) with the date of the beginning of the
suppression of his reign; this is unavoidable.
    When the program loads the file, it will report any unintelligible
entries.
    If you want to use file names other than CAL-FST.DAT and CAL-REG.DAT, you
may.  To get the program to load your files, simply put them on the command
line after the program name, regnal years first.  For example, if you had
special files relevant to Sicily, you might call them SIC-FST.DAT and 
SIC-REG.DAT; you would get the progam to load them by typing: 

    CALENDAR SIC-REG.DAT SIC-FST.DAT

at the A> prompt.

